<h1>Case studies</h1>
<p class="intro">
Some work doesn't split cleanly into one deliverable — it's a handful of quick, separate fixes that add up to a bigger story. This is where those stories live in full, rather than split across separate pages.
</p>

<section class="case-study">
<h2><a href="/case-study-1">The Greensman</a></h2>
<p class="project-meta">Freddy Trounce &middot; Elstead, Surrey &middot; Ongoing</p>
<p>
Freddy runs The Greensman, a garden and grounds maintenance business in Elstead. The work with him started as two small, separate quick wins, and has since grown into an ongoing tidy-up of the whole business's public face.
</p>
<p>
The first was financial: his existing website was on Wix's Light plan, costing him over £100 a year between hosting and the domain. Moving it to a self-hosted setup takes a bit of technical work but no ongoing subscription — so from next year, that's £100+ back in his pocket for no loss of website. <a href="/website-options">More on that here.</a>
</p>
<p>
The second was his print flyer, designed on Canva by someone else and handed over as a PDF. It had the wrong website address on it and made no mention of his autumn leaf-clearing offer — both spotted just by staying in regular contact with Freddy, not from a formal review. Both fixed in minutes in Claude Design, along with adding more of his growing collection of five-star reviews. <a href="/promoting-your-business">More on that here.</a>
</p>
<p>
Neither fix took long on its own, but doing both back to back surfaced something bigger: the flyer and the website had been built by two people who'd never spoken to each other, so small details — logos, fonts — had drifted apart. That's now its own piece of ongoing work: bringing the flyer and the rebuilt website into one consistent look, so everything Freddy hands a customer looks like it comes from the same place.
</p>
<p>
None of this was one big project. It's what happens when someone keeps a proper eye on a small business's moving parts — a website, a flyer, a growing pile of reviews — and fixes what's actually wrong, quickly, rather than waiting for a bigger brief.
</p>
</section>

